sections.body:
The diagnostic thresholds come from the World Health Organization, as set out by Diabetes UK:
- HbA1c 48 mmol/mol (6.5%) or more;
- fasting plasma glucose 7.0 mmol/L or more;
- plasma glucose 11.1 mmol/L or more two hours after a 75 g oral glucose load;
- random plasma glucose 11.1 mmol/L or more with typical symptoms.
Without symptoms a raised result is confirmed by a repeat test.
NICE PH38 defines high risk of type 2 diabetes (often called 'prediabetes', a term PH38 itself does not use) as HbA1c 42–47 mmol/mol (6.0–6.4%) or fasting plasma glucose 5.5–6.9 mmol/L. NICE NG28 covers management and does not set the diagnostic thresholds.
HbA1c reflects average glucose over roughly the previous 2–3 months. It is unreliable when red-cell lifespan is altered (haemolysis, recent blood loss or transfusion, some haemoglobinopathies and anaemias) and is not suitable for diagnosing rapidly developing diabetes, where glucose is used. HbA1c % (NGSP) ≈ 0.0915 × mmol/mol + 2.15, so 48 mmol/mol ≈ 6.5% and 53 mmol/mol ≈ 7.0%.
